# Authored as a CONTROL and it came back GUILTY, which is the finding.
#
# In go, `fmt.Print("x", "y")` recovers: a conversion takes one operand, so a
# second argument kills the wrong reading and the call survives. The same move
# in python does not work.
#
#   joints                      tree-sitter
#   print_statement [0, 11)       call [0, 11)
#     "print"                       function: identifier  "print"
#     argument: tuple [5, 11)       arguments: argument_list [5, 11)
#       "(" x "," y ")"               identifier x
#                                     identifier y
#
# Python 2's print statement takes a tuple, so it accepts every arity a call
# accepts. There is no input that starves the wrong reading to death. Where go
# can be rescued by the shape of the input, python cannot be rescued at all -
# the tie must be broken by rank, and `keyword_identifier`'s `prec -3` is
# nowhere near enough against `print_statement`'s `prec 1`.
#
# That difference is why the three "declared conflict" defects in this lane are
# not one fix. go's fork resolves itself on most real code; python's never
# does, and every bare `print(...)` in a Python 3 file is misread.
roots 1
mends 0
holds call
holds argument_list
lacks print_statement
lacks tuple
spans call 0 11
spans argument_list 5 11
